text:
Yep. If you move the mutex to the same shared scope as counter, both threads will contend on the same mutex, so the critical section becomes mutually exclusive.

For your code, this:

C
long counter = 0;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

should be outside worker(), and then each thread does:

C
pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
counter++;
pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

Your current file defines counter globally but the mutex locally inside worker(), so each thread gets its own lock object. mutex_racy

One tiny correction: with ITERATIONS set to 1,000,000 and two threads, the expected result is 2,000,000, not 200,000. mutex_racy

So after moving the mutex, you should see:

Expected: 2000000
Actual:   2000000

That scope detail is the entire bug.

