text:
Ah — the important point is: you normally wouldn't implement a spinlock using a mutex. A spinlock and a mutex are two different ways to provide the same higher-level property: mutual exclusion.

So the same program using a mutex is simply:

C
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdio.h>

#define ITERATIONS 1000000

long counter = 0;

pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void *worker(void *arg)
{
    for (long i = 0; i < ITERATIONS; i++) {

        pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

        counter++;

        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    }

    return NULL;
}

int main(void)
{
    pthread_t a, b;

    pthread_create(&a, NULL, worker, NULL);
    pthread_create(&b, NULL, worker, NULL);

    pthread_join(a, NULL);
    pthread_join(b, NULL);

    printf("Expected: %d\n", ITERATIONS * 2);
    printf("Actual:   %ld\n", counter);

    return 0;
}

Compare the critical part directly:

C
// mutex
pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
counter++;
pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

versus:

C
// homemade spinlock
spin_lock();
counter++;
spin_unlock();

They provide essentially the same logical guarantee:

only one thread at a time may execute counter++.

The difference is what happens to the loser.

With our spinlock:

C
while (atomic_flag_test_and_set(&lock)) {
    // keep trying
}

the losing thread remains running:

Thread B:
try → fail
try → fail
try → fail
try → fail
...

With a pthread_mutex_t, conceptually:

Thread B:
try → unavailable
       ↓
possibly sleep/block
       ↓
wake later
       ↓
try again

That's why I said atomics sit underneath locks conceptually:

             mutual exclusion
              /           \
             /             \
        spinlock          mutex
           |                |
      keeps spinning    may block/sleep
           \                /
            \              /
          low-level atomic state
Could you literally make a mutex spin?

Interestingly, yes. POSIX gives you:

C
pthread_mutex_trylock(&lock);

which says:

Try to acquire it, but don't wait if somebody already owns it.

So you could write something goofy like:

C
void weird_spin_lock(void)
{
    while (pthread_mutex_trylock(&lock) != 0) {
        // spin
    }
}

Then:

C
weird_spin_lock();

counter++;

pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

That would cause the thread to repeatedly attempt to acquire the mutex rather than blocking.

But there's usually no good reason to build it this way. You've taken a sophisticated blocking synchronization primitive and used it to imitate the simpler thing we already built from an atomic.

The interesting comparison is therefore not:

spinlock implemented with atomic
vs
spinlock implemented with mutex

but:

                 LOCKING STRATEGY

        Spinlock                  Mutex
           ↓                        ↓
    atomic acquisition       mutex acquisition
           ↓                        ↓
     if unavailable           if unavailable
           ↓                        ↓
      KEEP RUNNING          possibly BLOCK/SLEEP

And the thing both produce is:

Thread A        protected region       Thread B
   │                                    │
   ├──── owns lock ────► counter++      │
   │                                    │ waiting
   └──── releases ──────────────────────┤
                                        │
                             counter++ ◄─┤ owns lock

So our homemade spinlock wasn't meant to replace the earlier mutex example. It was meant to open the box one layer:

atomic operation
      ↓
build a lock bit
      ↓
build mutual exclusion
      ↓
protect ordinary data

That is why atomic_flag_test_and_set() is so revealing: you can actually see how a tiny indivisible operation is enough to construct the larger abstraction you were previously just handed as pthread_mutex_lock().

The next really useful question is probably: why does atomic_flag_test_and_set() itself work across CPU cores? That takes us from C's abstraction down toward caches, cache coherence, and memory ordering.

