text:
Yep — and you accidentally discovered two additional concurrency lessons in the code you wrote before we even get to false sharing. 😄

The biggest one is that printf() inside the hot loops completely changes the experiment. printf is enormously slower than an atomic increment, and stdout itself has internal synchronization so multiple threads don't corrupt the stream. So this:

C
atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&a, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
printf("B: %ld\n", b);

is no longer mainly measuring:

atomic A vs atomic B

It's much closer to:

Thread A ─┐
          ├── fight over stdout's internal lock
Thread B ─┘

You've hit the classic measurement problem:

Observing a concurrent system can perturb the scheduling you're trying to observe.

Your various printf placements therefore alter the thread timing enormously, which explains much of the bizarre apparent synchronization/desynchronization.

There's also a sneaky C bug in main():

C
atomic_long a = 0;
atomic_long b = 0;

/* ... */

int main(void) {
    pthread_t a, b;

Those local pthread_t a, b variables shadow your global atomic variables.

So at the end:

C
printf("A: %ld\n", a);
printf("B: %ld\n", b);

you are not printing the counters at all. You're printing the pthread_t values named a and b. Rename them:

C
pthread_t thread_a, thread_b;

That's a wonderfully nasty little example of why systems experiments need boringly clear variable names.

Also, when you write:

C
if (b > a)

you're implicitly loading both atomic objects. If the experiment is specifically about memory_order_relaxed, I'd make those loads explicit too:

C
long av = atomic_load_explicit(&a, memory_order_relaxed);
long bv = atomic_load_explicit(&b, memory_order_relaxed);

That way we always know which semantics we're asking C for.

Here's the actual false-sharing experiment

And yes, false_share was just a hypothetical filename I hadn't actually given you yet. Let's make a complete one.

This single program runs three cases:

1. SAME
   Both threads increment the same atomic.
   → real contention

2. FALSE SHARING
   Each thread increments a different atomic,
   but both atomics occupy the same cache line.
   → no logical contention, but hardware contention

3. SEPARATE
   Each thread increments a different atomic
   on a different cache line.
   → minimal coherence contention

Save as cache_pingpong.c:

