Compile with:

Bash
gcc -O2 -std=c11 -pthread cache_pingpong.c -o cache_pingpong

Then:

Bash
./cache_pingpong

Notice that we deliberately do not print from the workers.

The workers do almost nothing besides:

C
atomic_fetch_add_explicit(...)

That's intentional. We're trying to make the cache behavior dominate the experiment.

Look closely at the addresses

The program prints them deliberately.

For false sharing, you should see something approximately like:

2. FALSE SHARING
  address A: 0x...c080
  address B: 0x...c088
  distance : 8 bytes

Those are separate C objects:

a
b

but something like:

cache line
0x...c080 ───────────────────────── 0x...c0bf
    a        b
    ▲        ▲
    │        │
 Thread A  Thread B

Both cores need write ownership of the same cache line.

So hardware sees contention even though your program logically says:

Thread A never touches b
Thread B never touches a

That's the experiment.

For the separated version, you should see:

3. SEPARATE CACHE LINES
  address A: 0x...c100
  address B: 0x...c140
  distance : 64 bytes

Now:

cache line 1                    cache line 2
┌──────────────────┐           ┌──────────────────┐
│       a          │           │       b          │
└──────────────────┘           └──────────────────┘
        ▲                               ▲
     Thread A                        Thread B

Core A can keep ownership of its line while Core B keeps ownership of its own.

That's what we want.

The prediction before you run it

Don't worry about exact numbers. They depend enormously on your CPU, scheduler, virtualization, etc.

But conceptually I expect:

SAME ATOMIC
slow
because both threads genuinely fight over one location

FALSE SHARING
also surprisingly slow
because the cache line ping-pongs

SEPARATE CACHE LINES
substantially better
because the cache lines can remain independently owned

The striking comparison isn't even necessarily:

same vs false

It's:

false sharing
       vs
separate

because at the C level these programs are doing essentially identical work:

C
Thread A → increment A
Thread B → increment B

The only important change is:

8-byte separation
vs
64-byte separation

That's microarchitecture leaking through the abstraction.

Then perf

Once you've run it normally:

Bash
perf stat ./cache_pingpong

You can try:

Bash
perf stat \
    -e cycles,instructions,cache-references,cache-misses \
    ./cache_pingpong

Although an important caveat: generic cache-misses does not directly mean coherence ping-pong. Cache coherence has more specialized CPU-specific PMU events.

If your machine/kernel supports it, the much cooler tool is:

Bash
perf c2c record ./cache_pingpong

followed by:

Bash
perf c2c report

c2c means cache-to-cache, and it is specifically designed to help identify cache lines bouncing between cores. Availability can be limited inside VMs/containers, so don't be surprised if your sandbox refuses access to those counters.

And your printf discovery actually gives us a very good methodological rule for this entire project:

Instrumentation is part of the system.

Adding:

C
printf(...)

doesn't merely reveal an execution.

It adds locks, syscalls/buffering behavior, memory accesses, timing changes, cache activity, and scheduler opportunities.

In concurrency debugging this can get so extreme that adding a debugging statement makes a race disappear. Those timing-sensitive bugs are sometimes called Heisenbugs because observing them changes their behavior.

Which is hilariously appropriate for a systems course being done by somebody with a physics background.

