Yes — that makes sense, and I can format code that way going forward.

What you’re reacting to is basically loss of structural density. When something simple like:

C
pthread_create(&thread_a, NULL, worker_a, NULL);

becomes:

C
pthread_create(
    &thread_a,
    NULL,
    worker_a,
    NULL
);

you haven’t gained any useful conceptual separation, but you’ve spent five lines of vertical space. In a systems example where the important thing is seeing the relationship between:

C
sem_wait(&turn_a);
counter++;
sem_post(&turn_b);

that extra vertical expansion actively makes the control flow harder to hold in your head.

So I’ll use the compact style you showed:

C
void *worker_a(void *arg){
    for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
        sem_wait(&turn_a);
        counter++;
        printf("A: %ld\n", counter);
        sem_post(&turn_b);
    }

    pthread_barrier_wait(&finish_line);
    return NULL;
}

and similarly:

C
pthread_create(&thread_a, NULL, worker_a, NULL);
pthread_create(&thread_b, NULL, worker_b, NULL);

rather than exploding straightforward calls vertically.

I’ll generally keep these rules for your code examples:

function signature + { on the same line

one logical operation per line

function arguments stay on one line unless the line genuinely becomes unwieldy

preserve blank lines for conceptual sections, not syntactic decoration

comments close to the operation they explain

optimize for seeing the whole function/control-flow shape at once

For the kind of thing we’re studying, I agree that this is better. When you're trying to reason about ordering and synchronization, being able to visually scan:

wait
modify
post

as a compact block matters more than adhering to some maximally expanded formatter style.

I’ll stick to that style for the C examples from here on.

